# Architecture

How the four pieces of this repository fit together, and how to use them.

The three existing READMEs each document one layer in depth:
[`tools/README.md`](tools/README.md) for the build pipeline,
[`tools/LLM-Unit-Tests/README.md`](tools/LLM-Unit-Tests/README.md) for the test
suite, and
[`R9_1_GX_CLI_Reference/index/README.md`](R9_1_GX_CLI_Reference/index/README.md)
for which index file answers which query shape. This document sits above them
and explains the shape of the whole thing. Where a detail is settled elsewhere,
this document points at it rather than restating it.

## The problem

The Nokia 1830 GX Release 9.1 CLI Reference Guide is a 1,358-page PDF converted
to a single 28,634-line Markdown file. It documents 374 operation commands plus
21 auxiliary, navigation and piped commands. It is a reference work organised
for a human who already knows what they are looking for: it is a flat list of
command sections in alphabetical order, and every relationship between commands
is either implicit in an AID key path or stated in prose somewhere else.

An operator asking a question does not know the command name. They ask "why
won't the amplifier come up", or "how do I stop the node collecting performance
data", or "what lives under an IPsec policy". Answering that from the raw source
means either loading 28,634 lines into a context window or grepping for words
the document may never use.

The repository turns that document into something an agent can search on the
operator's behalf, and then measures whether it actually works.

## The four pieces

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    SRC["<b>1. Source guide</b><br/>R9_1_GX_CLI_Command_<br/>Reference_Guide_001P4.md<br/>28,634 lines, read-only"]
    SPLIT["<b>2. Split corpus</b><br/>386 byte-exact slices<br/>one file per command"]
    IDX["<b>3. Generated index</b><br/>INDEX.md, topics.md,<br/>parameters.md, entities.md,<br/>commands.jsonl, and more"]
    TEST["<b>4. Test suite</b><br/>457 questions<br/>5 stored model runs"]
    CUR["<b>curated.py</b><br/>the only file<br/>edited by hand"]

    SRC -->|step1_split.py| SPLIT
    SPLIT -->|"step3, step4, step5"| IDX
    CUR --> IDX
    IDX --> TEST
    SPLIT --> TEST
    TEST -.->|"gaps found become<br/>new search terms"| CUR

    style SRC fill:#e8e8e8,stroke:#555
    style CUR fill:#fff3cd,stroke:#856404
    style TEST fill:#e3f2fd,stroke:#1565c0
```

The dotted arrow is the part that makes this more than a document converter.
The test suite does not just report a score: when a test fails to retrieve, the
diagnosis is almost always a word an operator would type that `curated.py` does
not contain. About 70 such gaps have been found and closed that way, including
ones nobody would have guessed, such as `fibre` reaching nothing because every
search term used the American spelling.

| Piece | Lives in | Written by | Changed by |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Source guide | `R9_1_GX_CLI_Command_Reference_Guide_001P4.md` | Nokia | Never |
| Split corpus | `R9_1_GX_CLI_Reference/0*/`, `99-acronyms/` | `step1_split.py` | Rebuild |
| Generated index | `R9_1_GX_CLI_Reference/INDEX.md`, `index/`, `NAVIGATION.md` | `step4`, `step5` | Rebuild, or edit `curated.py` |
| Test suite | `tools/LLM-Unit-Tests/` | Hand and `_authoring.py` | Directly |

## Piece 1: the source, and the one contract everything rests on

The source document is read-only input. No script in the repository writes to
it, and its defects are reproduced rather than repaired. `tools/README.md`
carries a list of 23 confirmed source defects found while reading the corpus,
grouped into four recurring classes, precisely so that nobody spends an
afternoon deciding they are conversion bugs.

The contract that makes everything downstream trustworthy is byte-exactness.
Strip the YAML frontmatter from all 386 content slices, concatenate them in
source order, and the result must equal the source file byte for byte.
`step1_split.py` verifies this on every build and fails loudly if it breaks.

This matters more than it sounds. It means that a quote found in a split file is
a quote from the guide, not from a paraphrase, and therefore that the test
suite's evidence quotes are anchored in the document rather than in the derived
index. A test can check the index against the guide instead of checking the
index against itself.

## Piece 2: the split

`step1_split.py` cuts the source on H1 and H2 boundaries by line number.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    SRC["source guide<br/>28,634 lines"]

    SRC --> FM["00-front-matter/<br/>5 files"]
    SRC --> C1["01-introduction/<br/>1 file"]
    SRC --> C2["02-using-the-cli/<br/>1 file"]
    SRC --> C3["03-auxiliary-and-help-commands/<br/>1 file, 4 commands"]
    SRC --> C4["04-navigation-and-display-commands/<br/>1 file, 7 commands"]
    SRC --> C5["05-piped-commands/<br/>1 file, 10 commands"]
    SRC --> C6["06-operation-commands/<br/>375 files: overview<br/>+ one per command"]
    SRC --> AC["99-acronyms/<br/>1 file"]

    FM --> V
    C1 --> V
    C2 --> V
    C3 --> V
    C4 --> V
    C5 --> V
    C6 --> V
    AC --> V

    V{"strip frontmatter,<br/>concatenate in order,<br/>compare to source"}
    V -->|"identical"| OK["build continues"]
    V -->|"differs"| FAIL["build fails"]

    style FAIL fill:#ffebee,stroke:#c62828
    style OK fill:#e8f5e9,stroke:#2e7d32
```

Each slice gets frontmatter recording `source`, `part`, `section` and
`source_lines`. Everything downstream reads structure back out of those
headers rather than re-parsing the source, which is why replacing the source
with a new revision only requires re-deriving the line boundaries in
`step1_split.py` and the invariants in `gxpaths.py`.

Chapter 6 is one file per command, which is the granularity that makes
retrieval sharp: reaching the right file out of 374 is a real result. Chapters 3
to 5 are one file per chapter, so a query only has to reach "the piped commands
file" out of three. That asymmetry is deliberate and its consequences are
recorded in the test README. Splitting those chapters per command was
considered and rejected, because it would disturb the byte-exact split for a
gain confined to 21 commands.

## Piece 3: the index

The index is what an agent searches. It is built by four steps that run in
order, each depending on the previous one's output.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    SPLIT["386 split files"]
    CH2["chapter 2<br/>Tables 32-34"]
    CUR["<b>curated.py</b><br/>16 domains<br/>57 topics<br/>460 search terms"]

    CH2 -->|step2_privileges.py| PRIV["build/privileges.json"]
    SPLIT -->|step3_extract.py| RAW["build/commands.raw.jsonl<br/>one structured record<br/>per command"]
    SPLIT -->|step3_extract.py| PAGES["index/pages.tsv<br/>1,358 pages"]
    SPLIT -->|step3_extract.py| TABLES["index/tables.tsv<br/>857 tables, 7 figures"]

    RAW --> S4["step4_build_index.py"]
    PRIV --> S4
    CUR --> S4

    S4 --> INDEX["INDEX.md<br/>every command once,<br/>under one domain"]
    S4 --> TOPICS["index/topics.md<br/>vocabulary to commands"]
    S4 --> PARAMS["index/parameters.md<br/>1,787 parameter names"]
    S4 --> ENT["index/entities.md<br/>294 AID prefixes<br/>+ 87 containment links"]
    S4 --> ACC["index/access-control.md<br/>who may run what"]
    S4 --> JSONL["index/commands.jsonl<br/>395 records, machine-readable"]

    SPLIT -->|step5_navigation.py| NAV["NAVIGATION.md<br/>source TOC rewritten<br/>to relative paths"]

    style CUR fill:#fff3cd,stroke:#856404
```

`step3_extract.py` does the extraction: summary, syntax verbs, AID pattern,
access mode, every parameter row, usage details, table numbers and page
citations. It cross-checks its own page arithmetic against the document's table
of contents, and all 374 derived page numbers must equal the page the TOC
states. That check is the reason to trust the page map.

`step4_build_index.py` joins those records with `curated.py` and the privilege
tables and writes the human-and-agent-readable index files plus
`commands.jsonl`, which holds 395 records across 377 distinct files: 374
operation commands, 10 piped, 7 navigation and 4 auxiliary.

### curated.py is the one hand-edited file

`curated.py` holds two things: a single primary domain for each of the 395
commands, and 57 topics of the form `(title, search_terms, commands)`. The
search terms are the point. They exist so that a query using words the document
never uses still routes, which covers "wavelength", "laser shutdown",
"loopback", "factory reset", "OSNR", "span loss" and about seventy more.

Domain and topic assignments are editorial judgement made by reading all 374
command descriptions, and `index/topics.md` says so in its own header. They are
not extracted from the document and must not be presented to a user as though
the document stated them.

### Containment, and why it is derived here rather than reused

`entities.md` maps an AID prefix to the command that addresses it. It also
carries a Containment section: 87 parent-to-child links across 50 parent
entities, derived from the AID key paths.

That section exists because a test found the gap. `141-ipsec-spd-entry.md`
never uses the word "proposal", so the nesting of `ipsec-sa-proposal` under it
was discoverable from the child upward and invisible from the parent downward.
The relation was already latent in `commands.jsonl` and simply never emitted.

One derivation subtlety is worth carrying forward, because reusing the obvious
function would have looked correct and fixed nothing. `_clusters.py` roots a key
path at its **first** placeholder, which is right for grouping a family. The
immediate parent is the **second-to-last** placeholder. Under the first rule,
`ipsec-sa-proposal` becomes a child of `ikev2-local-instance` and
`ipsec-spd-entry` gets no children at all, preserving the exact gap being
closed.

### The checks

Three check scripts run after the build and gate it.

| Script | What it proves |
| --- | --- |
| `check_consistency.py` | 34 assertions. Every number written into prose in `INDEX.md`, `index/README.md`, `index/parameters.md` and `index/access-control.md` matches the data. Every advertised AID belongs to its command. No command loses more than three parameter rows to the parser. Indexed tables match the List of Tables exactly. |
| `check_index_links.py` | 2,245 links and all 395 JSONL records resolve. |
| `check_navigation.py` | All 463 `NAVIGATION.md` links resolve to a file and an anchor. |

`build_all.py --check` runs all three without rebuilding, and exits non-zero on
any failure, so it is safe to gate on.

## Piece 4: the test suite

The suite asks a question the build checks cannot: not "is the index internally
consistent" but "does it get an agent to the right page, and does the answer
carry what the guide states". 457 tests, each a natural-language operator
question paired with an approximate reference answer, verbatim evidence quotes,
the files it should reach, and the facts an answer must contain.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    T["<b>457 tests</b> &nbsp;·&nbsp; 392 single-command, 65 multi-command"]

    T --> L0

    subgraph L0["<b>Layer 0: validate</b> &nbsp;·&nbsp; no model, runs first"]
        A["· every evidence quote appears verbatim in the file it cites<br/>· every expected file exists, with the right path case<br/>· the fact set discriminates: at most 4 of 377 files satisfy all of a test's facts<br/>· one fact is specific: it appears in under 5% of files<br/>· the reference answer passes its own test<br/>· the command name is a required fact"]
    end

    L0 -->|"457/457"| L1

    subgraph L1["<b>Layer 1: retrieval</b> &nbsp;·&nbsp; no model, deterministic"]
        R1["match the question text against topics.md search terms, INDEX.md command<br/>names, parameters.md parameter names and entities.md AID prefixes"]
        R1 --> R2["single: must reach its one file &nbsp;·&nbsp; multi: must reach its<br/><i>primary</i> file plus half the rest of the cluster"]
    end

    L1 -->|"456/456, 1 compound excluded"| L2

    subgraph L2["<b>Layer 2: facts</b> &nbsp;·&nbsp; needs a model, costs money"]
        F1["an agent answers the question from the corpus alone,<br/>forbidden to read anything under tools/"]
        F1 --> F2["each answer must contain every required fact,<br/>matched on word boundaries rather than raw substrings"]
    end

    L2 --> STORE["runs/run-NN/answers.jsonl &nbsp;·&nbsp; 5 stored runs,<br/>re-scorable for free after any test edit"]

    L3["<i>Layer 3: judging prose against the reference answer.<br/>Deliberately not implemented; it needs a model at run time.</i>"]

    style L0 fill:#f1f8e9,stroke:#558b2f
    style L1 fill:#e3f2fd,stroke:#1565c0
    style L2 fill:#fff3e0,stroke:#e65100
    style L3 fill:#fafafa,stroke:#bbb,stroke-dasharray: 4 4
```

Layer 0 is what stops a fabricated test from passing. Layer 1 is a deterministic
lexical simulation of what an agent does, so it needs no model and can run on
every commit. Layer 2 is the only layer that costs money, which is why every run
is stored: `compare_runs.py` re-scores all five stored runs against the current
tests, so editing a test never means paying for the model again.

### Why five runs

A single run is a point estimate, and it cannot tell a question the corpus
genuinely cannot answer from one that happened to come out badly. Those want
opposite responses, so the distinction is the whole game.

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    R1["run-01<br/>98.4%"] --> C["compare_runs.py<br/>re-scores all five<br/>against the working tree"]
    R2["run-02<br/>97.3%"] --> C
    R3["run-03<br/>98.2%"] --> C
    R4["run-04<br/>97.4%"] --> C
    R5["run-05<br/>97.6%"] --> C
    C --> OUT["failure rate per test<br/>= failures / runs that answered it"]
    OUT --> A["5 tests at 3 of 5<br/>the triage candidates"]
    OUT --> B["25 tests below that<br/>shading into noise"]
```

Two findings from that history are worth keeping, because both reversed a
decision that had already been made:

- **Two runs were not enough.** Three tests failed both run 01 and run 02 and
  were being reported as real; run 03 passed all three. They had agreed by
  chance. Anyone triaging that list would have spent a third of the effort on
  noise.
- **A test has a failure rate, not a persistent-or-noise label.** "Failed by
  every run" gets mechanically harder to satisfy with each run added, so it says
  less the more evidence there is. `multi-ipsec-policy-nesting` failed 01 and
  02, passed 03, failed 04.

Quote the spread and the per-test rate, never a single headline number.

### What the suite is worth, stated honestly

Layers 0 and 1 being green says nothing about whether an answer is any good.
What the tests actually bought is a gap-finding instrument for the index, and it
paid: around 70 real vocabulary gaps found and fixed, plus one structural gap in
the index that no vocabulary edit would have closed.

The counterweight is that the suite has repeatedly been measuring itself. The
run-01 audit found that a third of its misses were the fact list's fault, not
the corpus's: reference answers habitually end in a tail of volunteered detail,
and facts harvested from that tail demanded material no operator asked about.
The same defect has now been found five separate times in different clothing.
Read the runs as diagnostic, not as a grade.

The largest remaining limitation is that all 457 questions were written by the
same process that wrote the expected facts. Five runs have measured the corpus
against our own idea of what an operator asks.

## How an agent actually uses the corpus

This is the path the index is designed for, and the one the run prompt in
`tools/LLM-Unit-Tests/runs/prompt.md` describes to the answering agents.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    Q["operator question<br/>in their own words"]

    Q --> D{"what does the<br/>question give you?"}

    D -->|"a command name"| I1["INDEX.md<br/>every command once,<br/>grouped by domain"]
    D -->|"subject vocabulary<br/>'wavelength', 'MACsec'"| I2["index/topics.md<br/>57 topics,<br/>457 search terms"]
    D -->|"a parameter name<br/>'admin-state', 'tx-power'"| I3["index/parameters.md<br/>1,787 names"]
    D -->|"an AID<br/>'card-1-1'"| I4["index/entities.md<br/>longest-prefix match"]
    D -->|"'what lives under X'"| I5["index/entities.md<br/>Containment section"]
    D -->|"'who may run X'"| I6["index/access-control.md"]
    D -->|"a page or table citation"| I7["index/pages.tsv<br/>index/tables.tsv"]
    D -->|"filtering, joins,<br/>bulk analysis"| I8["index/commands.jsonl"]
    D -->|"an acronym you<br/>do not recognise"| I9["99-acronyms/99-acronyms.md<br/>797 entries, nothing routes<br/>to it: look directly"]

    I1 --> F
    I2 --> F
    I3 --> F
    I4 --> F
    I5 --> F
    I6 --> F
    I7 --> F
    I8 --> F
    I9 --> F

    F["<b>the command file</b><br/>06-operation-commands/NNN-name.md<br/>median 53 lines"]
    F --> READ["read the whole file.<br/>Do not grep fragments<br/>out of it."]
    READ --> ANS["answer with the guide's own<br/>identifiers, values, defaults,<br/>ranges and pre-conditions"]

    style F fill:#e8f5e9,stroke:#2e7d32
    style ANS fill:#e3f2fd,stroke:#1565c0
```

Three properties make this work in practice.

**Command files are short and self-contained**, median 53 lines. Reading a whole
file is cheaper and more reliable than grepping, and it is what the retrieval
notes tell the agent to do. A grep for a fragment loses the parameter table that
gives the fragment its meaning.

**`INDEX.md` lists every command exactly once** under one primary domain, while
`topics.md` is where cross-cutting membership lives, so one command can appear
in several topics. Use `INDEX.md` to enumerate, `topics.md` to discover.

**Access mode answers "why did my command fail"**. A `cand`-only command must be
run after `configure` and needs `commit` to take effect. That is in the
`access_mode` field of every record.

Two cautions carried over from measurement. The guide covers four chassis
variants (G31, G32, G34c, G42) and parameter ranges differ per variant, so read
the parameter table rather than assuming. And the guide contains **no
procedures**, so any ordering in an answer ("first do A, then B") is inference,
not documentation.

## The invariants

These are the rules that keep the pieces consistent. Each one has already caught
a real problem.

1. **The source is never written to.** Its defects are documented, not fixed.
2. **Everything under `R9_1_GX_CLI_Reference/` is generated.** Never edit it by
   hand; the next build overwrites it. To change an output, change the step that
   produces it or change `curated.py`.
3. **The split reassembles byte-identically to the source**, verified on every
   build.
4. **`curated.py` is the only file meant to be edited by hand**, and its
   contents are editorial judgement rather than extracted fact.
5. **Evidence quotes must appear verbatim in the file they cite.** Layer 0
   enforces it, so a fabricated test cannot pass silently.
6. **A failing retrieval test means `curated.py` is missing a word an operator
   would type.** It does not mean the test should be relaxed. It also does not
   license adding a term so generic it pollutes routing: the harness measures
   recall only and will not see the damage. `precision.py` exists to see it.
7. **A test's id is its contract**, and none of its hyphenated words are
   decoration. The `not` in `default-not-everything-resets` is the whole answer.

## Command reference

```bash
python tools/build_all.py
```

Rebuild everything, then run the three checks. Add `--check` to verify without
building, `--quiet` to trim the output. Runs from anywhere; paths resolve from
the script location. Python 3.12 or newer, no third-party packages.

```bash
python tools/LLM-Unit-Tests/run_tests.py
```

Validate the tests against the document, then score retrieval. Add `-v` for
every test, `--answers <file>` to score layer 2, `--prose` to list identifiers
used in answers but absent from the files those answers cite, `--render` to
regenerate `TESTS.md`.

```bash
python tools/LLM-Unit-Tests/precision.py
```

What the index drags in alongside the answer, attributed to the term that
produced it. `--terms` ranks every search term by the noise it adds, `--dead`
lists terms no question fires. This is what makes a `curated.py` edit
falsifiable.

```bash
python tools/LLM-Unit-Tests/compare_runs.py
```

Score all five stored runs against the current tests and report each test's
failure rate.

```bash
python tools/LLM-Unit-Tests/prepare_run.py run-06
```

Shard the questions for a new model run and print the dispatch prompt. Add
`--collect` once the shards are answered.

